WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.440
Here's Ruth Wilson as Mrs. Coulter

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.482
in "His Dark Materials,"

00:00:09.482 --> 00:00:12.224
acting alongside puppeteer Brian Fisher

00:00:12.224 --> 00:00:15.350
as her daemon, the golden monkey.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:19.710
Fisher has the intricate and
difficult job of walking,

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:24.520
talking, and even breathing
in sync with Wilson.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.230
For the series, production company Bad Wolf

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.430
utilized the talents of puppeteers

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.210
and visual effects artists
to create over 2,000 shots

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.713
as well as 50 different creatures.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.190
So why go to all this effort with puppets

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.080
only to eventually
render the animal in CG?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.580
And why was it that the VFX artists

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.625
needed to wrestle each other?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.620
Even though the animals
look incredibly real,

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.635
the only animal actor on
set belongs to Lord Boreal

00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:03.768
and is played by an actual snake.

00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:06.910
This is Russell Dodgson,
a visual effects

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.430
supervisor at Framestore,
who first had to cast

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.200
the series, looking at different animals

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.730
that would play humans'
external souls, the daemons.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.130
Russell Dodgson: It was very
clear that we wanted to go

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.620
for a naturalistic
performance for the daemons,

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.610
because we didn't want
them to have a kind of

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.760
caricature-ish or
cartoonish quality to them.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.050
Narrator: He worked with
pre- and post-visualization

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.283
company Painting Practice

00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:34.583
to look at animal reference images

00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:37.130
and brought in animal experts to advise

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.300
on the creatures' gender and their correct

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.890
groom and coloration.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.952
The VFX team then ran animation tests

00:01:44.952 --> 00:01:46.901
in facial-rig software

00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:49.870
with dogs sniffing and monkeys jumping

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.218
to try out the facial movement.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.440
They realized they needed
to perform dialogue

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.322
through the animals' bodies

00:01:57.322 --> 00:01:59.180
instead of just using their mouths

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.837
and facial expressions to emote.

00:02:01.837 --> 00:02:03.430
Dodgson: They would always
start with the rhythm

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.810
of their body and then
just add in the lip-sync

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.000
as almost like the final
touch, rather than saying

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.060
we've gotta get all of the shape phonemes

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.240
perfect every single time they speak.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.200
Narrator: Puppetry
legend William Todd-Jones

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.870
then devised a series of
proxy stand-ins, rigs,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.669
and puppets for the
actors to interact with.

00:02:22.669 --> 00:02:25.940
And this is where Brian comes back in.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.960
For close-ups, some of the
puppets were sculpted heads.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.970
Other slinky-body styles of puppet

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:36.420
for quadrupedal or bipedal animals.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.570
When actors needed to demonstrate
the weight of daemons,

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.690
the team made gray furry
cushions called bucks.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.598
These had weight and shape

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:48.973
to make it look like actors
were holding daemons.

00:02:48.973 --> 00:02:53.210
But why bother with this
complex, laborious process?

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.210
Dodgson: Puppeteering, I'm
giving the actors something

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.276
to play off of, was critical.

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:59.042
Because we're not just making kind of

00:02:59.042 --> 00:03:00.370
creatures that chase after people;

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.645
we're making parts of people.

00:03:02.645 --> 00:03:04.152
You know, we're representing their soul.

00:03:04.152 --> 00:03:05.610
It's really amazing how you can have

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.293
a very simple puppet face,

00:03:08.293 --> 00:03:09.765
and just the way that, like,

00:03:09.765 --> 00:03:11.275
a puppeteer just does a little head cock,

00:03:11.275 --> 00:03:13.588
and you suddenly just
throw emotion onto it.

00:03:13.588 --> 00:03:15.620
Narrator: Another
advantage of using puppets

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.820
was the "puppet pass."

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.670
Take this scene with Brian.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.400
This scene is blocked with
the puppets so the crew

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.180
and actors know where the daemon would be.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.000
They delivered dialogue
or sound to the actors

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.775
via the puppets to rehearse the scene.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.890
Brian makes noises like barks or clicks

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.520
or even breathes so Ruth, as Mrs. Coulter,

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.743
feels more in tune with her costar.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.700
They shoot the scene once with the puppet,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.423
and then with just an
eyeline, or nothing at all.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.720
The actor gets so used to
having the puppets there

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.193
that they then perform to the empty space.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.910
This allows the director to
use the take with the puppet

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:01.920
if the performance is better,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.147
as the VFX team could then
easily edit out the puppet.

00:04:06.147 --> 00:04:08.710
And this was often the case.

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:12.500
Now, that's all fine for
something as small as a monkey,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.990
but the team had to think
bigger for something

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:16.990
as sizable as a polar bear.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.269
Dodgson: For Iorek and the bears,

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:21.770
it was a whole different story.

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:24.510
Loads of rigs for them.
A full-size head sculpt

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.920
was measured so that there
was a stick attached to it

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.480
that when the performer, Joe Tandberg,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.957
put it on his belly button, it became

00:04:29.957 --> 00:04:31.273
the right height for Iorek's head.

00:04:31.273 --> 00:04:34.050
It gave you the two eyes
and the nose as a shape

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.370
and the full volume of
the head, which was great

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.010
because Iorek's really
imposing and you need someone

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.600
to feel like there's
something in their space.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.730
Narrator: And it wasn't
just creating space

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.110
that made the puppets so important.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.340
Puppets assisted the
action when actors needed

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.150
to be thrown around the
place or physically shifted.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.350
There was the "basher bear"
to ram into stuntpeople

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.538
and push actors to the ground,

00:04:58.538 --> 00:05:00.738
like in this scene with Harry Melling.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.860
There was also a riding
rig for Dafne to sit on,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.550
a backpack rig for her to get
on top of someone's shoulders,

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.680
and a four-legged
fiberglass standing puppet

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.280
to show a polar bear's height.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.880
But the team also had
to go further than using

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.540
this elaborate set of puppetry tools.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:23.150
In order to capture the bears'
movement, including fighting,

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:27.560
the VFX team used their own
bodies and wrestled each other.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.592
They filmed themselves grappling,

00:05:29.592 --> 00:05:32.440
using mixed martial arts references,

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.310
as the way bears wrestle
is quite similar to humans.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.360
Painting Practice then
built the entire bear palace

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.747
in the "unreal engine" game simulator,

00:05:42.747 --> 00:05:45.840
and the team filmed the fight in that.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.360
To live-track the set, they used a tool

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.465
called Vanishing Point vector,

00:05:50.465 --> 00:05:53.412
which is a brand-new piece
of tracking software.

00:05:53.412 --> 00:05:57.181
It leverages augmented
reality and real-time tracking

00:05:57.181 --> 00:06:01.300
by placing a camera sensor on
top of the main unit camera.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.880
So the bears were real-time
on the viewfinders

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.860
for the camera operators.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.610
Dodgson: We could have made
the entire bear palace in CG.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:11.310
But because we had so much
time in there with the actors,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.230
we knew that we wanted that to be a space.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.410
With any technology,
there's always the chance

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.460
of something going wrong or
you wanting to completely

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.450
adapt to a different thing
or change the animation,

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:21.659
which you can't do live there, and then,

00:06:21.659 --> 00:06:23.237
so we also then would just
bring the puppets out,

00:06:23.237 --> 00:06:26.702
which are kind of true,
tested, analog way of doing it,

00:06:26.702 --> 00:06:29.620
and we'd bring them out and
work through it that way.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.110
Narrator: Then began the
painstaking process of CGI

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.670
detail for the bears and daemons.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.010
It took 700 days just to make polar bear

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.513
Iorek Byrnison and his armor.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:45.247
Amazingly, they didn't
find bears the hardest

00:06:45.247 --> 00:06:47.670
individual animal to make.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:50.060
Birds, such as The Masters' daemon,

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.160
were some of the trickiest.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.800
The point where the wings come together

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.390
and interlink is also tricky in 3D.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.860
A computer finds it
difficult to process them

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.630
coexisting in a similar space.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.333
So the team had to use puppets

00:07:04.333 --> 00:07:07.872
to land on actors' arms or take off

00:07:07.872 --> 00:07:10.960
so they had a better visual
reference for the CG.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.355
Even though you see over 50 puppeteered

00:07:13.355 --> 00:07:16.129
and CGI animals in the final product,

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:19.200
the amount of daemons in
the final pass was limited.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.880
The big character moments
remain, but having

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:25.750
too many background animals
was deemed distracting.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:30.750
So, what you see is the bare
essentials, formed, ironically,

00:07:30.753 --> 00:07:34.261
through a painstaking,
multi-department process.

